    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup for **Ghost Ride the WHIP** is anchored by a high-upside trio in the outfield, led by **Corbin Carroll** (80.95), **Seiya Suzuki** (75.03), and **James Wood** (70.10). **Corbin Carroll** enjoys a prime matchup against Germ\u00e1n M\u00e1rquez, where his elite sprint speed and the projected 5.64 xERA of the opposing starter position him for a massive day despite a minor penalty for the pitcher\u2019s hold potential. **Seiya Suzuki** boasts the day's highest park factor boost (+16%) and a favorable matchup against Baz, though his 75.03 score is tempered by a 45% rain risk. Meanwhile, **James Wood** is our most efficient catalyst; hitting out of the leadoff spot against Imai, he benefits from an elite 0.424 xwOBA projection and a slow delivery environment that favors his stolen base upside. We also see strong value from **Brice Turang** (61.87) and **Kyle Karros** (60.47), both of whom are projected for high-leverage lineup spots and benefit from optimal platoon splits.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Max Muncy**, who finished with a 44.60 score\u2014just shy of our preferred thresholds. While **Max Muncy** carries upside, he was squeezed out of the utility and corner infield slots by the higher projected efficiency of **Josh Naylor** and **Travis Bazzana**. The optimizer ultimately favored the latter due to a combination of favorable platoon splits and, in **Travis Bazzana**\u2019s case, the value of the leadoff spot. We are also monitoring **Gabriel Moreno**, whose 66.56 score is contingent on confirmed lineup status, but his favorable matchup against the hittable Germ\u00e1n M\u00e1rquez makes him a high-floor play at catcher. With the rain concerns surrounding **Seiya Suzuki** and the overall depth of the roster, we remain vigilant, keeping **Austin Riley** and **Isaac Paredes** in reserve to capitalize on late-game shifts if weather forces a shakeup.",
